# The Library tracks itself as software. Reader material and runtime state stay out,
# because a checkout that recovers a script must never roll back the reader's knowledge.

# Reader material — must not roll back
shelf/
notebook/
raw/
# output/ joined them on 2026-09-19 (PLAN-public-release.md step 13). It had been tracked
# since before the program and the workspace were separate, which made the repository carry
# the reader's own deliverables; ADR-0027 names it as reader material by the workspace's own
# definition. The tester-packet sources that lived under it moved to the Librarian 2.0 repo.
output/

# Application-managed records — journals are evidence of writes that really happened.
# internal/book-locks/ is transient runtime state and is covered by this rule too.
internal/

# Virtual Desk runtime state. The two loose files are the pre-seat layout, kept ignored so a
# checkout that predates the migration does not start tracking them; seats/ is where a Desk lives
# now, one directory per seat, and it belongs to this checkout rather than to the code.
.claude/.open-books
.claude/.open-projects
.claude/seats/

# The deployment this checkout is attached to: the Basic Memory endpoint and the collection id.
# Generated by tools/Initialize-CodexLibrary.ps1 and resolved by tools/LibraryDeployment.ps1.
# Tracked until 2026-09-19, when the endpoint left fourteen files and the collection id seventeen:
# a committed deployment is one reader's network address travelling in everyone else's clone.
# `.library-project` keeps the exact one-line contract the reader adapter has always read.
.claude/.library-project
.claude/.library-mcp-url
.claude/.library-shared-root

# Which just-in-time instructions a session has already been served. Runtime state keyed by
# session id: it means nothing in another checkout and nothing after the sessions in it have ended.
.claude/.hook-served.json

# Raw hook payloads, written only while this directory exists. Creating it is how the field sets in
# .claude/hooks/payload-contract.json are re-measured after a client upgrade; the payloads carry the
# reader's own tool inputs and paths, so the directory is never tracked and never exported.
.claude/hooks/.capture/

# Machine-local overrides. A global ignore rule also covers this, but relying on
# it means the protection is absent on any other checkout.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Generated Codex bindings. The tracked templates are portable; the initializer
# writes these files with absolute paths for the current copy of the Library.
.codex/config.toml
.codex/hooks.json
.codex/*.bak

# Codex review scratch
codex-verdict.txt

# Desk state. Application-managed record of which Books and Projects are open;
# it belongs to this checkout, not to the code. Tracking it would let a restore
# roll the reader's Desk back along with a revert.
.library/

# Aside copies left by the cutover protocol. tools/Move-LibraryFolder.ps1 renames a
# moved source to <name>.aside rather than deleting it, and the archive purge -- its
# own gated operation -- is the only thing that removes one. When the source sits
# INSIDE this working tree, that copy lands here as untracked files, so a `git add -A`
# would commit the very material the run just archived. Ignored rather than deleted:
# the aside copy is what a rollback restores.
*.aside/

# The TypeScript kernel's dependency and build trees (PLAN-public-release.md step 28). Both are
# re-derivable from kernel/package.json and neither is product; tools/PublicTreeAllowlist.ps1 denies
# the same two paths so an export cannot carry them either.
kernel/node_modules/
kernel/dist/
